  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/5d90e9eb29955babde9d3af1ccd690b9d6b8fae6c07ad14da3867318b8487d90.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous?",
  "worked_solution": "The function is continuous on the open intervals  $(-5,0),$ and  $(0,2\\pi ).$ \n\nAdditionally, the function is right-continuous at $x=0$ and at $x=-5.$  \n\nTherefore, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $[-5,0)$  and  $[0,2\\pi ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $[0,\\pi ]$ \n\nNotice that:\n\n- $[0,2\\pi ]$ and $[\\pi ,2\\pi ]$ contain the point $x=2\\pi ,$ but the function is not defined at this point, so it can't be continuous on these intervals.\n\n- $[-5,0]$ and $[-2,\\pi ]$  contain the point of discontinuity $x=0,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals.",
